@page "/"

<PageTitle>ModbusTools</PageTitle>

<h1>ModbusTools</h1>

<p class="lead">
    Tools for working out what sits on a Modbus RTU (RS-485) bus and how it is set up, running entirely in the
    browser. They drive a serial port through the Web Serial API: nothing to install, and nothing leaves the machine.
</p>

<p>
    Every tool only reads. They send read and identification requests, never a write, so they are safe to point at a
    live device — bearing in mind that reads themselves are not always free of side effects, since some devices clear
    counters or drop queued events when those addresses are read.
</p>

<div class="row g-3 mt-1">
    <div class="col-lg-4">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">
                    <a class="stretched-link text-decoration-none" href="scanner">Slave ID Scanner</a>
                </h2>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Finds which addresses answer on a bus whose serial settings are already known. It probes every ID
                    in a range with one read request and sorts what comes back into replies, exceptions, garbled
                    frames and silence, then hands the settings straight to the other tools.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-lg-4">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">
                    <a class="stretched-link text-decoration-none" href="crawler">Register Map Crawler</a>
                </h2>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Maps which addresses exist in the data tables of one known slave, one address per request. The
                    result is the set of valid ranges and the values they held, exportable as CSV or as a draft
                    device profile.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-lg-4">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">
                    <a class="stretched-link text-decoration-none" href="baud-sweep">Baud Rate Sweep</a>
                </h2>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Measures how far the baud rate can drift before a device stops answering. The middle of the range
                    that still works is the rate the device really runs at; the distance to its edges is the room
                    left for clock drift.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 class="h4 mt-4">What you need</h2>

<ul>
    <li>A desktop Chromium-based browser — Chrome, Edge or Opera. Firefox and Safari have no Web Serial API.</li>
    <li>The page opened over HTTPS or on localhost, which the API requires.</li>
    <li>
        A serial port: a USB-to-RS485 or USB-to-serial adapter, or a built-in COM port. The browser asks for
        permission the first time each port is used, and remembers it afterwards.
    </li>
</ul>
